changelog: |
  0.1.0 — the fleet's model step and lane, versioned ONCE. Promoted from
  lifehacker.dev scripts/ai/run.sh + .github/actions/claude-run (PR #643), where
  a real fleet of 17 lanes tests it daily, after the 2026-09-06 review found
  three divergent copies of one `claude-run` contract (lifehacker, it-journey,
  zer0-mistakes — three hashes, one name) and two of them exiting 0 on a dead
  credential. The runner: model resolution (--model > AI_MODEL > the consumer's
  _data/ai.yml > fleet default), OAuth-first (`env -u ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`),
  Claude Code primary with an optional consumer-owned API fallback, optional
  consumer-owned metering, a post-run one-paragraph-per-line normalizer honouring
  the consumer's .prose-excludes, and the exit contract: 0 = ran or nothing was
  attempted, 1 = attempted and FAILED (with the reason, as a ::error::). The
  reusable lane adds the kill switch (`vars.<SWITCH>`, dispatch bypasses), the
  bot guard, named concurrency, probed GH_PAT → GH_TOKEN, runtime setup,
  pre/post hooks, and the result-file assertion. Ten contract tests.
notes: |
  Consumed by reference, so there is nothing for --upgrade to byte-compare:
  every consumer follows @main on its next run. The caller template IS a file a
  repo holds, so it is stamped and archived like agent-context; the runtime is
  not. Ruby is the only runtime the runner needs beyond bash (model resolution,
  result parsing) and ubuntu-latest ships it. Consumers that vendored the
  runner (scripts/ai/run.sh) prove parity with
  `AI_RUNNER_SUT=scripts/ai/run.sh bash tests/contract.sh`.
